#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073590 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b09eb59-3ad5-5cde-8503-3c28c9e5c546" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Multiple Transformations to a Point Using Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "6b45326c-ce13-5d2f-84df-b3698b46ea68" :question/math-academy-id "e-2888" :question/problem "The point $Q(4,3)$ is shifted $2$ units down, reflected across the $y$-axis, and then shifted $1$ unit to the right. What is the resulting point?" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the functional representations of our three transformations:\n\n- A shift of $2$ units down can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (x,y-2).\n$$\n\n- A reflection in the $y$-axis can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (-x,y).\n$$\n\n- A shift of $1$ unit to the right can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto (x+1,y).\n$$\n\nLet's apply the given transformations to the point $Q$ using our functions:\n\nFirst, we apply the shift. The image of the point $\\left(4,3\\right)$ under the shift is\n\n$$\n(4,3) \\mapsto  (4,3-2)=(4, 1) .\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we apply the reflection. The image of the point $\\left(4,1\\right)$ under the reflection is\n\n$$\n(4, 1) \\mapsto(-4,1) .\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the second shift. The image of the point $\\left(-4,1\\right)$ under the shift is\n\n$$\n(-4,1) \\mapsto (-4+1,1)=(-3, 1) .\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(-3,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073591 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4308331-fea0-51e1-b1c9-a3e8e122e09e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Parameters Given a Point and Its Image Under Multiple Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "b45dae26-7996-5544-9884-d604bac62993" :question/math-academy-id "e-2893" :question/problem "The point $P(5,14)$ is reflected across the line $y=x$ and then stretched according to the function $(x,y)  \\mapsto (x,ky).$ If the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations is $(14,25),$ then what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\nA reflection across the line $y=x$ can be represented by\n\n$$\n(x,y ) \\mapsto (y, x) .\n$$\n\n \nApplying this to the point $P,$ we get\n\n$$\n(5,14 ) \\mapsto (14,5) .\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, applying the stretch to $(14,5),$ we get\n\n$$\n(14, 5) \\mapsto (14,  5k) .\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the resulting point is $(14,25),$ by equating the $y$-coordinates, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k=25\\,⟹\\,k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073648 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9322724c-215a-5a91-a478-e91abe4038da" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Polygon Under the Action of Multiple Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "14fc5fcd-4aaa-5c46-a991-4703ceb0434c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7747" :question/problem "![](images/70/707fabf08d90a67f39a89c27b1bb12f475447d63a86ef0867fbae1fd0d80eaf9.png)\n\nThe triangle shown above is rotated by $90^{\\circ}$ counterclockwise about the origin and then reflected across the $x$-axis. What is the resulting triangle?" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the given transformation.\n\n- Rotating the triangle by $90^{\\circ}$ counterclockwise about the origin gives the following:\n\n![](images/fa/fae3cc7c804b24b6d6c35a5910963377683c3249e4b3c8f1440cc2338265ddd7.png)\n\n- Next, we take the last result and reflect it across the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/ee/ee29eebc26c9a852b5f58cc0f544c8c713f0cdae043cff519d3974639c8cb48a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073650 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1614303a-a067-5879-9ba6-2e7505109136" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Point Given a Sequence of Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "0f05c408-7ea2-58d2-a8e3-663f819747d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7793" :question/problem "The point $P$ below is rotated by $45^\\circ$ counterclockwise around the origin and then reflected across the $x$-axis. Which of the given points is the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations?\n\n![](images/eb/eb4416a7d6a0d131ad6b535571001b61a50152c59459bf125f42dff2788411cd.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\n- Rotating the point $P$ by $45^\\circ$ counterclockwise around the origin gives the point $A,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/53/53128ffb3404a6fb27b096896f744b57e143ec2c5d630ae7ef9d80c256bfb43c.png)\n\n- Reflecting the point $A$ in the $x$-axis gives the point $D,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/23/234d8273aea8cfc59810b44f575887de0effe09d7ab95657b4d8b76baf822a04.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $D.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1614303a-a067-5879-9ba6-2e7505109136" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Point Given a Sequence of Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eee0a65-5ed2-56db-ba3f-d48df409d4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536307} {:answer/id #uuid "15208e74-1d31-51ba-93e8-cf82981974c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "E" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536316} {:answer/id #uuid "82631aad-ab73-5d0c-8bcd-d7e1de109ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536497} {:answer/id #uuid "ae64aabd-a453-588c-83a1-6093db748efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536565} {:answer/id #uuid "f81a5cc8-6100-502d-a626-1be458495be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae64aabd-a453-588c-83a1-6093db748efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536565} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba67288a-19a5-589f-a458-3e46ebe362e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abf3e34b-133a-5d9e-997c-b480aa675d59" :question/math-academy-id "q-126556" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9ecdc9de8ef21f4d585c93f55a96a240ecda723bfe824878cc126cd93e70b4f7.png)\n\nThe point $P$ shown above is reflected across the $y$-axis and then translated $5$ units down.  Which of the given points is the image of $P$ under this sequence of transformations?" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the given transformations.\n\n- Reflecting the point $P$ across the $y$-axis gives the point $A,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/dd/dd85f6229eb27b535707a138ec89f49185e88e764465b925fcb4fe30b45fda29.png)\n\n- Shifting the point $A$ down by $5$ units gives the point $C,$ as shown  below:\n\n![](images/1b/1b4f60b0ebb8b0a99ebad30ef549bc95faf1e5d650de83e994fcad4f71ee76ad.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $C.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9322724c-215a-5a91-a478-e91abe4038da" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Polygon Under the Action of Multiple Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "147ffdc0-24a2-5d99-94c8-4dc089c3bee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ac/ac4fe75b19d85ea0e78ffcb08c440054805b9cc5fd1f91bf2cffa3d22b160507.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536315} {:answer/id #uuid "1b719625-bf60-5411-8eb1-a8436